'''Cecilio Ríos International Airport''' ({{lang|ca|Aeroport Internacional Cecilio Ríos}}; [[IATA airport code|IATA]]: SBT, [[ICAO airport code|ICAO]]: SBIB) is the principal airport serving [[Sant Bart]] and the [[Jala Province|Jala highland region]] in [[Balboa]]. Located in [[Zona 8 (Sant Bart)|Zona 8]] in the southern portion of the city, at an elevation of 950 metres, the airport is the busiest in Jala Province and the second-busiest in Balboa after [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport]]. It is a hub for the flag carrier [[Aeroístmus]] and the low-cost carrier [[RapidAir (Balboa)|RapidAir]].

The airport is named after [[Cecilio Ríos]], a Sant Bart lawyer who served as the fourth [[List of presidents of the Republic of Balboa (1899–1973)|president of the Republic of Balboa]] from 1956 to 1962. Ríos negotiated the [[Twelve Guarantees]] with the [[Adonian Community]], signed the [[Treaty of Balboa Harbour]] (1960) providing for the return of the Sant Cristòfor mandate, and enacted an economic liberalisation programme that laid the groundwork for the post-reunification economy. A native of Sant Bart, Ríos is regarded as one of the most consequential figures of the late rump state period.

The airport's highland location at 950 metres elevation requires longer runways than sea-level airports of comparable size, due to reduced air density affecting aircraft performance on takeoff and landing.

== History ==

=== Early aviation and the rump state (1932–1947) ===

The first airfield at Sant Bart was established in 1932 as a grass strip on the southern edge of the city, during the rump state period. The rump state, having lost [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the [[Balboa Canal]] to [[Zong]] in the [[Second War of the Isthmus]], was cut off from the Shendan coast and relied on the overland route to the Aethiopian coast for trade and communication. The rump state government established the airfield to provide an air link to the outside world, supplementing the slow and unreliable overland route.

The original airfield consisted of a single grass runway, a small wooden terminal building, and a hangar. The first regular air service, operated by a private company under government contract, connected Sant Bart to the Aethiopian coastal plain with twice-weekly flights using light aircraft. The route was primarily used for mail and government correspondence, with occasional passenger seats.

During the [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947), the airfield was requisitioned by the Zong occupation forces and used for military purposes. The runway was paved for the first time in 1941 to accommodate Zong military aircraft, and a control tower was erected. The airfield sustained damage during the final stages of the war.

=== Post-war reconstruction (1948–1972) ===

After the Great Adonian War, the rump state government rebuilt and expanded the airfield. A new terminal building was constructed in 1952, and the paved runway was extended to 2,000 metres to accommodate the first generation of commercial turboprop aircraft. Regular scheduled services were established to destinations within the rump state, and the first international route — to [[Ampuria]] operated by [[Volisanian Airlines]] — was inaugurated in 1958, reflecting the continuing commercial ties between Balboa and the former colonial power.

The airport was renamed '''Cecilio Ríos International Airport''' in 1978, as part of the post-reunification modernisation programme. The renaming honoured [[Cecilio Ríos]], the fourth president of the Republic of Balboa (1956–1962), who had negotiated the [[Twelve Guarantees]] with the [[Adonian Community]] and signed the [[Treaty of Balboa Harbour]] (1960) providing for the return of the Sant Cristòfor mandate. Ríos, a native of Sant Bart, was regarded as one of the most consequential figures of the late rump state period, and his economic liberalisation programme was widely credited with laying the institutional groundwork for the post-reunification economy. The renaming was part of a broader post-reunification effort to honour figures from the rump state who had contributed to the process of reconciliation and reunification.

=== Post-reunification modernisation (1973–2000) ===

The reunification of Balboa in 1973, following the [[Treaty of Areza]], transformed the airport's role. Sant Bart lost its status as the national capital, and the airport was no longer the principal international gateway of the rump state. However, the airport continued to serve as the principal airport of Jala Province and the Jala highland region.

The [[Balboa Airports Authority]] was established in 1975 to oversee the modernisation of the country's airport infrastructure. At Sant Bart, the authority oversaw the construction of a new terminal in 1978, designed to handle the first generation of jet airliners. The primary runway was extended to 3,000 metres in 1982 to accommodate wide-body aircraft, and a second parallel runway was added in 1989 to separate domestic and international operations. A third runway (06/24), oriented perpendicular to the parallel pair at the southern end of the airport, was constructed in 1996 to provide crosswind capability.

The first jet-era international routes were established in the 1980s, with [[Aeroístmus]] operating services to [[Sant Cristòfor]] and other domestic destinations, and [[Volisanian Airlines]] maintaining the Ampuria route. The airport's passenger traffic grew steadily through the 1980s and 1990s, driven by the economic growth of the Sant Bart metropolitan area and the development of the Jala highland region.

=== International expansion (2000–present) ===

The primary runway was further extended to 3,400 metres in 1998, enabling the airport to handle long-haul wide-body international flights. The extension was necessary due to the airport's highland elevation, which requires longer runways than sea-level airports for the same aircraft types. The terminal was expanded in 2005 to increase capacity, and the apron was enlarged to accommodate additional aircraft stands.

[[Aeroístmus]] expanded its international route network from Sant Bart in stages: a weekly service to [[Guledga Airport|Guledga]] was inaugurated in 2010, and in 2022 the airline added twice-weekly flights (Mondays and Fridays) to [[Concordia International Airport|Castejón]] and [[Okami-Tobu International Airport|Okami-Tobu]].

In 2014, a modernisation programme was completed, including the renovation of the terminal building, the installation of new baggage handling systems, and the upgrade of navigation aids to [[Instrument Landing System|ILS]] Category II on the primary runway. The airport handled 6.8 million passengers in 2025, making it the second-busiest airport in Balboa.

== Infrastructure ==

=== Terminals ===

The airport has a single terminal building with two concourses:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Concourse !! Function !! Gates !! Annual capacity
|-
| A || Domestic || 12 || ~4 million
|-
| B || International || 10 || ~4 million
|-
| '''Total''' || '''22''' || '''~8 million'''
|}

The terminal was originally built in 1978 and was expanded in 2005. Concourse A handles domestic flights within Balboa, while Concourse B handles international flights. The two concourses share a central check-in and baggage claim area. The terminal has 22 gates in total, with 12 domestic and 10 international gates.

=== Runways ===

The airport has three runways. The two parallel runways (15R/33L and 15L/33R) are aligned approximately parallel to the [[Great Eastern Line|Great Eastern rail corridor]], which passes through the southern portion of the city. The third runway (06/24) is perpendicular to the parallel pair, crossing both at the southern end of the airport:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Direction !! Length !! Surface !! Use
|-
| 15R/33L || 3,400 m || Asphalt || Primary; all commercial passenger operations, including wide-body international flights
|-
| 15L/33R || 3,000 m || Asphalt || Secondary; domestic, general aviation, relief
|-
| 06/24 || 2,600 m || Asphalt || Crosswind; domestic, general aviation
|}

Runway 15R/33L is the primary runway, handling all commercial passenger operations. At 3,400 metres, it is one of the longest runways in Balboa, a length necessitated by the airport's elevation of 950 metres — at this altitude, reduced air density increases takeoff and landing distances by approximately 15–20 per cent compared to sea level. Runway 15L/33R, added in 1989, is used for domestic services, general aviation, and as a relief runway during peak periods. Runway 06/24, oriented perpendicular to the parallel pair at the southern end of the airport, provides crosswind capability and is used primarily for domestic operations and general aviation. All three runways are equipped with [[Instrument Landing System|ILS]] Category II approach systems.

=== Cargo facilities ===

The airport has a dedicated cargo area on the western side of the apron, with warehousing and logistics facilities operated by the [[Balboa Airports Authority]] and private logistics companies. The airport handled 48,500 tonnes of cargo in 2025. Cargo operations primarily serve the Jala highland region, including industrial goods, agricultural products, and perishables.

